(** * SpendAuthG fixed-base window-point table: builders and materialised literal

    The 85 x 8 = 680 Weierstrass multiples of the SpendAuthG generator that both
    SpendAuthG certificates read:

    - the x-coordinate Lagrange certificate
      ([circuit_proof/spend_auth_g/x_cert.v]);
    - the positive QR window-sign certificate
      ([circuit_proof/spend_auth_g/sign_cert.v]).

    [full_table] builds the multiples through the scalar-multiplication
    composition law (Weierstrass [mul_mul]), whose bridge lemmas in
    [circuit_proof/ladder/main.v] relate this concrete computation to the
    abstract multiples [mul (window_scalar 85 w d) G].  Concretely:

    - [FixedBaseTableDefs.nonlast_points 84 G] builds the 84 non-last windows incrementally: window
      [w]'s row is [[(d+2)] B_w] for [d = 0..7] (i.e. [[2]B_w .. [9]B_w]), with
      [B_w = [8^w] G] threaded as the 7th row entry [[8]B_w = B_{w+1}], so the
      whole 84*8 = 672 window points share one octupling chain;
    - the last window (84) is [[d * 8^84 - FixedBaseTableDefs.window_offset_sum 84] G] for
      [d = 0..7], assembled from [B_84 = [8^84] G] ([base_pow8]) and the offset
      point [[FixedBaseTableDefs.window_offset_sum 84] G].

    [full_table_reduced] is the same table as a pasted affine-coordinate
    literal, generated once offline by [Eval vm_compute in full_table]; the
    equality [full_table_reduced_eq] is the single heavy [vm_compute] [Qed] of
    the SpendAuthG certificate stack (the octupling chain, evaluated once by
    the kernel's VM).  The two certificate files read only the literal, so
    their own [vm_compute]s cost seconds.

    This file depends only on the elliptic-curve layer (Weierstrass/Pallas),
    the SpendAuthG generator point ([Orchard.Pallas.Generators]), and the
    shared table builders ([circuit_proof.table_defs]):
    iterating on [Field/Sqrt.v], [ecc/chip/window_disc.v],
    [ecc/chip/fixed_window_canonical.v], or the rest of the Orchard proof
    stack never invalidates it, so the heavy [Qed] re-runs only when the
    curve layer or the generator point itself changes. *)

